update progressnote 
set description = '<p>Safety & Well-being
 
Prior to the visit, Ms. Johnson was asked the following questions: You or anyone in the household: Has fever, cough, shortness of breath, nausea, vomiting, or diarrhea; Is currently sick with COVID-19 or the flu; or Has been told by a health provider that they should not have visitors due to illness. Ms. Johnson is fully vaccinated and answered no to all the questions.
 
This worker completed a monthly visit with Mylo Shan along with foster parent, Paureta Johnson. Social distancing was practiced, and face masks were worn. Upon this worker’s arrival, Mylo was eating pizza for lunch. He was happy to see this worker as he displayed a smile and said, “hi”. Mylo appeared to be safe, clean, and healthy.
  
 Mental & Physical Health
 
Mylo appears to be healthy and is up to date with his medical appointments. Ms. Johnson continues to receive nutrition vouchers from the Women Infants and Children''s (WIC) Program of Prince Georges County for Mylo. The next appointment is August 2, 2021.
 
Progress towards Permanency Goal
 
Mylo was found CINA on October 7, 2020 and his permanency plan is reunification. Carmin Shan, mother, has had minimal contact and shown unwillingness to cooperate with the Department. The Department makes every concerted effort to communicate with Ms. Shan regarding the needs of her son, visitation and court ordered services. There have been a couple of instances where Ms. Shan has made commentary in reference to clothing and food pertaining to Mylo. Ms. Shan was advised to address any questions or concerns with the foster parent directly as she is his primary caretaker so they can have a positive working relationship. In February 2021, Ms. Shan delivered Ma’Cheira. Since then, the Department received three reports of medical neglect regarding Ma’Cheira. One was made from the hospital where Ms. Shan gave birth and the other two were from Children’s National Medical Center. In speaking with Ms. McKay, Ms. Shan left St. Ann’s as a result of the last Child Protective Services (CPS) report that was made on May 11, 2021.
  
On May 13, 2021, it was reported that Ms. Shan did not return to her placement, St. Ann’s Center for Children, Youth & Families in Hyattsville, Maryland after a day pass. As a result, a missing person’s report (#PP21051300002093) was filed through the Prince George’s County Police Department by Officer Rosa, Badge #3456. The following day, May 14, 2021, Ms. Shan sent St. Ann''s staff an email saying, “she and her daughter, Ma’Cheira are fine and that she will be by the agency to meet with the police and gather some of her belongings”. When she arrived, her daughter was not with her. The police were called and stated that they would close her case via telephone (due to COVID safety rules) since her return had been confirmed. Ms. Shan plans on returning on May 19, 2021, to pick up her remaining items. She reported that she will live with Ma’Cheira’s aunt, Sharonitta Myles, who Ms. Shan refers to as her “sister in law”. in Washington, D.C. but will continue to work with the Department to get her Family Unification Program (FUP) housing voucher. It should be noted that Ms. Shan is in jeopardy of losing her FUP due to missing documentation. At this juncture, her whereabouts are unknown
 
At this time, the Department has been unable to verify Ms. Shan’s employment status due to her not providing documentation. She was last employed with Costco Wholesale in Washington, D.C.
 
Ms. Shan is linked to A Better Tomorrow Starts Today (BTST) for mental health services. She is assigned to Olubunmi Akinkuowo, LCSW for individual therapy. On March 15, 2021, she was sent a Consent for Release of Information to sign in order for the Department to communicate and monitor services with BTST. However, she declined to sign the form. On April 9, 2021, Ms. Shan finally signed the document. As of May 26, 2021, Ms. Akinkuowo reported that Ms. Shan has been actively engaged in therapy and is working through past trauma and adjusting to her parenting role. Her next session is scheduled for May 31, 2021. She completed her psychological evaluation with Center for Therapeutic Concepts, Inc. (CTC) on April 17, 2021.
 
Due to Ms. Shan’s history with sex-trafficking, she was linked to Courtney’s House. However, she stopped engaging in services so foster care worker Januari McKay referred her again on November 5, 2020 and followed up on January 27, 2021 to obtain an update on resumption of services. It should be noted that Ms. McKay as well as former St. Ann’s social worker, Nicole Terry, LMSW experienced challenges making contact with Courtney’s House. According to Ms. McKay, Ms. Shan continued to decline services but expressed she was open to receiving the services through FAIR Girls in April 2021. Although she was willing to begin services with FAIR Girls, Ms. McKay had already referred her on March 15, 2021. On April 19, 2021, it was reported that the Case Management Coordinator with FAIR Girls made contact with Ms. Shan, but she declined their services. On April 20, 2021, Ms. McKay reported speaking with Ms. Shan, and she expressed that she is looking forward to hearing from someone from FAIR Girls. At this juncture, the Department has been unable to receive a status update regarding Ms. Shan’s engagement in services. An email was sent on May 14, 2021 and May 19, 2021.
 
Ms. Shan’s court case in Howard County was held on April 8, 2021, and she was placed on unsupervised probation. She was expected to have her fingerprints completed with parole and probation within 3 days. On May 5, 2021, Ms. Shan had not been fingerprinted, and indicated that she was awaiting guidance from her attorney on when she can get it done. To date, she has not been fingerprinted.
 
Concerning visitation, Ms. Shan had been participating in 30-minute virtual visits twice a week. Family Support Worker, Beauford McKinney supervises these visits. Mr. McKinney reports that he has observed Ms. Shan to be fully engaged. She keeps Mylo interested by talking to him about his drawings and books, singing songs, and playing games. On May 3, 2021, in-person visits began and were held from 11:00 a.m. to 12:00 p.m. That day, she was a no show. A visit did not occur on May 10, 2021 due to Mr. McKinney being on leave so foster parent, Paureta Johnson supervised the virtual visit. Ms. Johnson noted during the visit on May 12, 2021 that she heard a guy in the background, but he never spoke to Mylo. On May 17, 2021, the visit was cancelled. At 10:52 a.m., Mr. McKinney reported that Ms. Shan had just contacted him and stated that her schedule has changed so it will need to be rescheduled. She has not been in contact with the Department for visits to occur at this time.  
  
Mylo continues to participate in virtual visits with his sister, Melody and great grandmother, Argie Head. Their last in-person visit was on June 5, 2021. 
 
Placement
 
Mylo continues to reside in the Prince George''s County Department of Social Services licensed foster home of Paureta Johnson in Laurel, Maryland. He has adjusted well. He continues to have his own room and sleeps in a toddler bed, which is within calling distance of his foster mom’s bedroom. Mylo is thriving very well and has made exceptional progress. He is actively potty training.  Mylo was quite hesitant in the beginning but is now progressing well.  He, for the most part, reports prior to going or states he needs to use the bathroom.  However, Mylo still has accidents when it comes to number two, but he is definitely progressing. He wears pull ups and has had many successful nights of not wetting himself while sleeping but there are nights that are not successful but fewer than the successful ones. 
 
Employment & Education
 
Mylo continues to stay with Ms. Johnson during the day as she works from home. He receives virtual in-home based education two times a month, with Special Educator, Kimberly G. Johnson. Mylo has a Transition Planning Meeting scheduled for June 21, 2021 as his 3rd birthday is approaching. At this time, the enrollment packet has been completed for the Pre-K Program through Prince George''s County Public Schools.
 
Benchmarks and Life Skills (Youth 14 and Older)
 
N/A
 
If the youth identifies as LGBTQ, are they receiving supportive services around their sexual orientation and/or gender identity.
 
N/A 
 
If the youth is pregnant or parenting, are they receiving supportive services? (If yes, list the services within the agency or community)
 
N/A
 
If the youth has history of sex trafficking/exploitation, are they receiving supportive services? (If yes, list the services within the agency or community)
 
N/A
 
Supportive Adults
 
Mylo''s supports are his foster parent, mother, and maternal family.

</p>',
updatedon = now(),
updatedby = 'CDM-14373'
where progressnoteid = 'ce30fbac-2995-4096-9662-aeb4b063cec5';



